        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "@justinmk,\r\n> why is the call in normal_finish_command insufficient?\r\n> ...\r\n> ideally this would be handled at a higher layer like that, rather than a mouse-specific patch.\r\n\r\n`curwin` is reassigned to window under mouse pointer (the one which doesn't have focus), so moving this logic to `normal.c` means we have to duplicate getting inactive window logic from `mouse.c`\r\n\r\n@igorlfs,\r\n> I think this is intended behavior (which I also dislike). From the scroll-binding docs:\r\n> \r\n> > When using the scrollbars or the mouse wheel, the binding only happens when\r\n> > scrolling the window with focus (where the cursor is). You can use this to\r\n> > avoid scroll-binding for a moment without resetting options.\r\n\r\nRight, this one was taken from `vim` originally. But I'd say that's a counterintuitive behavior:\r\n- if we can use mouse to move around window which doesn't have a focus just to check context, why the window which _does_ have a focus is treated differently?\r\n- when inactive window was scrolled to some position, scrolling active window resets this position immediately causing visual jumps and loosing information about scroll position in inactive window (that was the reason which motivated me to suggested change in the first place). The problem gets even worse if more than 2 windows are bound together (like in case of 3-way merge).\r\n\r\nSo, do we want this behavior to be changed? If so, should I create PR for `vim` first?",
            "created_at": "2026-09-26T09:24:47Z",
